B. THE LAYER FIT — two components, two silicon points
==============================================================================
    0.8B  inv    5,160,960  var    271,360  measured 15.128 ms
    2B    inv    5,160,960  var    493,568  measured 17.960 ms
    A  (invariant, DN state + conv) = 2.261123e-06 ms/element = 0.5653 aclk cycles/element
    Bv (H/FFN-proportional)         = 1.274482e-05 ms/element = 3.1862 aclk cycles/element
    split at 0.8B: invariant 11.670 ms (77.1 %), variable 3.458 ms (22.9 %)
    *** READ THE SPLIT AS AN INTERCEPT, NOT A COST. *** Both
    calibration points share DeltaNet geometry, so `A` is the
    INTERCEPT of a two-point fit: it is whatever is left after the
    H/FFN term explains the 0.8B->2B difference. Nothing measured
    the DeltaNet contribution directly. What IS primary-source is
    that the work it stands for scales with nDN x LNH: rtl/dn_step.sv
    :1,:13-14 (one head per invocation, ~1300 cyc/head, 128 lanes),
    rtl/layer_chan.sv:393 (ONE instance), :891 (head is a command
    arg), ref/gen_layer_script.py:954-961 (the per-head emit loop).
    For scale, the DNST commands alone at 1300 cyc/head and 250 MHz:
      0.8B/2B  18 x 16 = 288 cmd/token -> 1.498 ms  (9.9 % of the 0.8B layer term)
      4B/9B    24 x 32 = 768 cmd/token -> 3.994 ms

