C. THE STEP MODEL — (r, overlap) solved from the TWO silicon steps
==============================================================================
    R-d re-measured the layer term UPWARD (MOVER_NORM used build_033's
    14.130 ms; R-d's L_LCYC census on build_035 reads 15.128).  With
    that term a SERIAL sum over-predicts the 0.8B step, which is direct
    evidence for what MOVER_NORM 3.2 flagged as possible but could not
    resolve: at nch=4 some mover work HIDES under engine time (no-wait
    MVGO, 217 FENCEs/token).  So the model carries one overlap factor:

        step = beats_busiest * r / UI  +  movers_counted * m  +  layer

    and (r, m) are solved from the two measured steps.  Nothing else
    is fitted; both come out physically admissible, which is the test.

    0.8B W4: matvec 5.4754*r + movers 11.332*m + layer 15.128 = 31.5023
    2B   W8: matvec 25.3651*r + movers 15.969*m + layer 17.960 = 60.5145
    -> r = 1.1037 ui-cyc/beat  = 17.40 GB/s/chan  (INSIDE MOVER_NORM 3.2's calibration-free bracket [1.0, 1.131])
    -> m = 0.9117  i.e. 8.8 % of counted mover work overlaps engine time at nch=4
       (MOVER_NORM 3.1's independently derived r was 1.0892; this solve lands +1.3 % from it)
    Both are admissible: r >= 1.000 is the engine's hard floor and
    0 <= 1-m <= 1 is required of an overlap.  Neither was constrained.

    --- HONESTY CHECK 1: the calibration-free bracket, RE-ANCHORED ---
    MOVER_NORM 3.2(b) derives r <= 1.131 from
        45.018 = 14.13 + matvec_1 + movers_1,  movers_1 >= 6.308
    but 14.13 is build_033's layer term, which R-d SUPERSEDES with
    15.128 (RD_GATE 3).  Re-running that same inequality:
      layer  14.130  (MOVER_NORM's 14.130 (build_033))
        matvec_1 <= 24.580 ms  ->  r <= 1.1310   (16.98 GB/s/chan)
      layer  15.128  (R-d's 15.128 (build_035))
        matvec_1 <= 23.582 ms  ->  r <= 1.0851   (17.70 GB/s/chan)
    => the bracket becomes [1.000, 1.0851], and this study's
       r = 1.1037 is **OUTSIDE it by 1.7 %**.
    An earlier revision claimed r landed INSIDE MOVER_NORM's bracket.
    That was true only against the bracket's SUPERSEDED anchor, and
    it is withdrawn.  MOVER_NORM:161-165 also states its own point
    estimate is soft: r rests on a bubble figure of 8.252 where the
    repo's other figure is 8.94, and 8 % on the bubble moves r 7 %
    (to 1.013).  So neither r=1.0892 nor the bracket is a hard rail.

    --- HONESTY CHECK 2: nch=1, a configuration NOTHING here was fitted to ---
      counted nch=1 busiest beats 6,522,432 (MOVER_NORM 2: 6,522,432)  OK
      counted nch=1 movers 7.306 ms (MOVER_NORM 3.3: 7.293)
      m = 1.000 (MOVER_NORM: at nch=1 nothing overlaps)
        predicted step 46.422 ms vs MEASURED 45.018  -> +3.1 %
      m = 0.9117 (this study's nch=4 solve)
        predicted step 45.776 ms vs MEASURED 45.018  -> +1.7 %
    The model OVER-predicts nch=1 by 1-3 %.  Reported, not hidden:
    the (r, m) pair is exactly determined by two nch=4 equations, so
    it has no residual AT those points and cannot be 'validated' by
    them; nch=1 is the only out-of-sample test available and it does
    not close.  Consequence for this study: the ABSOLUTE tok/s carry
    a few per cent of unmodelled configuration dependence on top of
    the layer allowance, and the RELATIVE 4B-vs-9B comparison — which
    shares r, m and the mover law — is much the sounder of the two.

